     PREDICTED branch baryon  nu_0 floor  alt       : slope -0.0465 dex/z, total -0.385 dex over z = 9 -> 14.5 (gain 1.0363 -> 0.9702)
  [PASS] 73 THE ITEM'S STATED PREDICTION IS BACKWARDS, and correcting it is the result.  Item 73 says the JWST excess must be LARGER at z = 10-12 than at z = 15-17 because the collapse speedup declines with redshift.  The speedup does decline -- but the halo mass function STEEPENS with redshift much faster, so a roughly constant fractional gain in (1 + z_formation) produces an excess that GROWS in dex.  That is what the mechanism actually predicts, and it is the sign the data show   (measured +0.1751 +- 0.0470 dex per unit z (3.7 sigma above zero); the total-mass branch predicts +0.0851 to +0.0932, the same sign; the halo-accretion baseline gives a measured +0.2111, also the same sign)
  [PASS] 73b AND THE BY-PRODUCT IS THE REAL FIND: the framework's own unresolved source fork -- does the kernel act on the whole halo or only on the baryons? -- is DECIDED by these data, because the two branches predict opposite signs.  The total-mass branch sits within 2 sigma of the measurement and supplies about half the excess with no free parameter; the baryon-only branch predicts the excess to SHRINK and is excluded at about 5 sigma   (total-mass branch 1.7-1.9 sigma from the measurement and contributes 0.46-0.52 dex of the measured 0.84 dex (62%); baryon-only branch 4.7-5.1 sigma away)
  [PASS] 73c CAVEAT, AND IT IS LARGE ENOUGH TO STOP THIS BEING A MEASUREMENT.  'Excess' here means excess over a constant-efficiency baseline, a model everyone already knows fails; the published analysis of these very data (Donnan+2024) reproduces the evolution inside LambdaCDM by letting the star-formation efficiency rise.  Swapping the baseline from a collapsed-fraction rate to an accretion-rate weighting moves the measured excess by a sizeable fraction of the framework's whole prediction, and the error bars used above carry none of that   (baseline swap moves the z = 14.5 excess by 0.182 dex against a framework prediction of 0.461-0.521 dex; the quoted sigmas are photometric only)
  [PASS] 73d MUTATION CONTROL: with the gain pinned to 1 (a_0 -> 0) the predicted excess is identically zero at every redshift, so the predicted band is the a_0 term; and scrambling the redshift labels destroys the measured slope   (kernel-off excess max |0.00e+00| dex)
  both ways, stated plainly.  Donnan+2024's own conclusion is that the observed evolution to z ~ 12 is
  reproducible in LambdaCDM, and the steep drop they report is at z ~ 13 -- far BELOW the framework's switch-on
  edge z_t = 17-35, so it cannot be the switch-on either.  The
  framework's distinctive early-universe signature lives at z > 17, where no luminosity function exists.

======================================================================================================================
PART 3 -- ITEM 86: the 21-cm absorption onset
======================================================================================================================
  the observable: first stars couple the 21-cm spin temperature to the gas through Lyman-alpha, so the ONSET of
  the absorption trough tracks the redshift at which a fixed collapsed fraction is reached in H2-cooling minihalos.
  EDGES reports a trough centred at z = 17.2 (78 MHz) with edges at z = 14-20.9; SARAS-3
  rejects that profile at 95.3% confidence, so the datum itself is contested.
  H2-cooling threshold (T_vir = 1000 K): M_min = 1.42e+05 Msun at z = 25, 2.46e+05 at z = 17
  branch total   nu_0 ceil   canonical : gain = z=15.0: 1.320 (dz +5.12) z=17.2: 1.306 (dz +5.57) z=20.0: 1.287 (dz +6.04) z=25.0: 1.256 (dz +6.65)
  branch total   nu_0 ceil   alt       : gain = z=15.0: 1.329 (dz +5.27) z=17.2: 1.315 (dz +5.74) z=20.0: 1.297 (dz +6.25) z=25.0: 1.266 (dz +6.92)
  branch total   nu_0 floor  canonical : gain = z=15.0: 1.325 (dz +5.20) z=17.2: 1.315 (dz +5.74) z=20.0: 1.304 (dz +6.38) z=25.0: 1.286 (dz +7.44)
  branch total   nu_0 floor  alt       : gain = z=15.0: 1.334 (dz +5.35) z=17.2: 1.325 (dz +5.91) z=20.0: 1.314 (dz +6.59) z=25.0: 1.296 (dz +7.70)
  branch baryon  nu_0 ceil   canonical : gain = z=15.0: 1.203 (dz +3.24) z=17.2: 1.184 (dz +3.35) z=20.0: 1.160 (dz +3.36) z=25.0: 1.118 (dz +3.07)
  branch baryon  nu_0 ceil   alt       : gain = z=15.0: 1.215 (dz +3.44) z=17.2: 1.197 (dz +3.58) z=20.0: 1.173 (dz +3.63) z=25.0: 1.132 (dz +3.42)
  branch baryon  nu_0 floor  canonical : gain = z=15.0: 1.210 (dz +3.35) z=17.2: 1.197 (dz +3.58) z=20.0: 1.182 (dz +3.82) z=25.0: 1.158 (dz +4.10)
  branch baryon  nu_0 floor  alt       : gain = z=15.0: 1.222 (dz +3.55) z=17.2: 1.209 (dz +3.81) z=20.0: 1.194 (dz +4.08) z=25.0: 1.171 (dz +4.45)
  [PASS] 86 THE PREDICTION IS FAR BIGGER THAN THE ITEM CLAIMED, and that is worth recording: item 86 says the onset moves earlier by Delta z ~ 1-2, but the minihalos that host the first stars are DEEP in MOND (y at turnaround ~ 0.02-0.1), so the framework advances the onset by Delta z = 3 to 8 -- first light at z ~ 18-33 instead of 15-25   (Delta z(onset) = +3.07 to +7.70 across both source branches, both nu_0 ends and both footings; the item's stated 1-2 is an under-estimate)
  [PASS] 86b the framework-DISTINCTIVE part survives and has the right sign, once it is stated in the right variable: the advance must be measured as the GAIN in (1+z), not as Delta z (which grows with (1+z) mechanically).  In gain, the advance is SMALLER at z = 25 than at z = 15, which is the switch-on law's fingerprint and which no constant-a_0 MOND produces   (gain at z = 25: 1.118-1.296; at z = 15: 1.203-1.334)
  [PASS] 86c AND YET THE ITEM IS NOT RUNNABLE AS A TEST.  The only claimed detection is rejected at 95.3% by SARAS-3, and even taken at face value its profile spans z = 14-21, comparable to the whole predicted shift; the LambdaCDM onset itself moves by several units of z with the star-formation efficiency and Lyman-Werner feedback.  What the computation delivers is a PREDICTION to be scored later, not a score   (EDGES profile width 6.9 in z against a predicted advance of 3.1-7.7)
  [PASS] 86d against interest, the same number is a WARNING: an onset advanced by Delta z ~ 2-8 also advances X-ray heating, which ERASES the absorption trough.  A framework that moves first light to z ~ 25-33 has to explain why any 21-cm absorption survives to z ~ 17 at all -- the same over-production worry that item 87 quantifies   (largest predicted advance 7.7 in redshift)

