PART 4 -- ITEM 87: the reionization optical depth (the one that can bite)
======================================================================================================================
  comoving n_H = 0.191 m^-3; Planck 2018 tau_e = 0.0544 +- 0.0073
  LambdaCDM baseline calibrated: ionizing efficiency zeta = 13.04 gives tau_e = 0.0544 (target 0.0544); midpoint z(Q = 0.5) = 6.74, end z(Q = 0.99) = 4.63
  [PASS] 4a AGAINST INTEREST, THE BASELINE MODEL'S OWN LIMITATION, STATED FIRST: a single-efficiency reionization model tuned to Planck's tau reionizes too gradually -- the ionized midpoint lands near z ~ 7 but the last neutral gas is not cleared until z ~ 4-5, later than the Lyman-alpha forest allows (z ~ 5.3).  The item's output is a DIFFERENCE of two histories computed the same way, which is far less sensitive to that than either history is, but the absolute tau values below inherit it   (z(Q = 0.5) = 6.74 (acceptable), z(Q = 0.99) = 4.63 (too late; the forest says ~5.3))
  branch total   nu_0 ceil   canonical : gain(z=10) = 1.2754 -> collapsed fraction x2.40 -> tau_e = 0.0700 (+2.1 sigma), midpoint z = 8.32
  branch total   nu_0 ceil   alt       : gain(z=10) = 1.2856 -> collapsed fraction x2.46 -> tau_e = 0.0703 (+2.2 sigma), midpoint z = 8.36
  branch total   nu_0 floor  canonical : gain(z=10) = 1.2762 -> collapsed fraction x2.40 -> tau_e = 0.0700 (+2.1 sigma), midpoint z = 8.31
  branch total   nu_0 floor  alt       : gain(z=10) = 1.2863 -> collapsed fraction x2.46 -> tau_e = 0.0703 (+2.2 sigma), midpoint z = 8.35
  branch baryon  nu_0 ceil   canonical : gain(z=10) = 1.1439 -> collapsed fraction x1.69 -> tau_e = 0.0644 (+1.4 sigma), midpoint z = 7.81
  branch baryon  nu_0 ceil   alt       : gain(z=10) = 1.1572 -> collapsed fraction x1.76 -> tau_e = 0.0650 (+1.4 sigma), midpoint z = 7.87
  branch baryon  nu_0 floor  canonical : gain(z=10) = 1.1449 -> collapsed fraction x1.69 -> tau_e = 0.0644 (+1.4 sigma), midpoint z = 7.80
  branch baryon  nu_0 floor  alt       : gain(z=10) = 1.1582 -> collapsed fraction x1.76 -> tau_e = 0.0650 (+1.4 sigma), midpoint z = 7.86
  [PASS] 87 A REAL, QUANTIFIED LIABILITY.  Holding the ionizing efficiency at the value that makes LambdaCDM reproduce Planck's tau_e, the framework's earlier collapse reionizes the universe too early in EVERY branch, at both ends of the nu_0 window and on both footings.  The framework must therefore run a LOWER ionizing efficiency than LambdaCDM by a definite factor -- allowed, because the efficiency is not independently measured that well, but a cost that was not on the ledger before   (tau_e = 0.0644 to 0.0703 against Planck 0.0544 +- 0.0073, i.e. +1.4 to +2.2 sigma; the collapsed fraction at z = 10 is raised x1.69-2.46 and the ionized midpoint moves to z = 7.8-8.4)
  [PASS] 87b MUTATION CONTROL: a gain pinned to 1 must return the LambdaCDM baseline exactly, and it does, so the tau shift is the collapse speedup and not an integrator drift   (tau(gain = 1) = 0.05440000 vs baseline 0.05440000)
  [PASS] 87c and here is the size of the cost, stated as the thing an observer could check: to land on Planck's tau_e the framework needs an ionizing efficiency lower than LambdaCDM's by the factor below.  JWST's own ionizing-photon budget (xi_ion times f_esc for the z = 6-9 population) is that measurement, and it is currently uncertain by a factor 2-3 -- so the item is a recorded cost today and becomes a kill the moment the budget is pinned to ~30%   (zeta(framework)/zeta(LambdaCDM) = 0.75 on the most conservative branch (baryon source, nu_0 ceiling, canonical footing); the total-mass branch needs a larger reduction still)

